\begin_layout Title
Set 3 - Controls: When and How
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Controls
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Selection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There is a case to be made for control variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Selection on observables: regressions that control for these variables have
 the potential to yield unbiased estiamtes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Selection on unobservables that are correlated with observables: Bias is
 reduced by controlling for these variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But, what are the approaches to controlling? First off, we need to make
 parametric assumptions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Approaches to Controlling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Exact matching is the ideal approach because it produces an unbiased estimate
 of the ATE.
 However, exact matching is not possible with a continuous, or high-dimension
 vector, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 of characteristics.
 Since we are unable to perform exact matching, there are accomodations,
 or technical 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fixes,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 that we can consider, such as: inexact matching; nearest neighbor matching;
 propensity score matching.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Regrettably, these 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fixes
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 are not very popular.
 There is a case to be made against complex approaches to controlling for
 selection bias when the selection process is very complicated.
 If we think the selection process involves many characteristics 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, then how likely is it that we observe all important variables in our data?
 It is not very likely.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is easier to deal with circumstances in which the selection process is
 more simple.
 So, it is more convincing to focus on special circumstances where most
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

usual sources of bias
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 are not present.
 Though, we may still have concerns about some sources of bias.
 We want to make sure that our estimates do not change when we control for
 these sources of bias.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Suppose selection into treatment depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},x_{4},x_{5}$
\end_inset

, i.e., the probability of treatment depends on these five variables.
 If we were to use a random sample of data, then we would have to control
 for these five variables, which could be very tricky.
 Moreover, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{6}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{7}$
\end_inset

 may also be relevant to the probability of treatment, but we do not observe
 them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Overcontrolling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Overcontrolling occurs by including variables in 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 that are affected by treatment.
 To illustrate, let us consider the following example where 
\begin_inset Formula $y=\mbox{earnings}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $T=\mbox{job training}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x=\mbox{employed}$
\end_inset

.
 Assuming ignorability implies that we could hold employment status fixed
 when considering the effect of treatment.
 Intuitively, this is nonsensical.
 Instead, we estimate the effect (with pure random assignment) conditional
 on employment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we have a regression controlling for 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, or we just restrict the sample to 
\begin_inset Formula $x=1$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $x=0$
\end_inset

.
 Right now, we are focusing on 
\begin_inset Formula $x=1$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E[y_{i}|T_{i}=1,x_{i}=1]-E[y_{i}|T_{i}=0,x_{i}=1] & = & E[y_{1i}|T_{i}=1,x_{1i}=1]-E[y_{0i}|T_{i}=0,x_{0i}=1]\\
 & = & E[y_{1i}|x_{1i}=1]-E[y_{0i}|x_{0i}=1]\ \ \mbox{(by RA)}\\
 & = & E[y_{1i}|x_{1i}=1]-E[y_{0i}|x_{0i}=1]+\left(E[y_{0i}|x_{1i}=1]-E[y_{0i}|x_{1i}=1]\right)\\
 & = & E[y_{1i}-y_{0i}|x_{1i}=1]+E[y_{0i}|x_{1i}=1]-E[y_{0i}|x_{0i}=1]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

This gives us the causal effect of training on those who will find employment
 after training, plus some junk.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The junk on the right represents a selection effect: the difference in expected
 non-treated earnings between those who would be employed with training
 and those who would be employed without training.
 So, even if there is no causal effect of training, the estimate will not
 tell us this.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
There is no causal interpretation to estimates that condition on intermediate
 factors that link treatment to outcomes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This example shows why overcontrolling does not give us what we want.
 Suppose that draft eligibility for the military is randomly assigned, but
 individuals can avoid military service by being in school.
 Thus, eligibility, which is randomly assigned, affects military service
 and education.
 Clearly, we can identify unbiased estimates of the effect of eligibility
 on anything (say earnings).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

But, suppose we are interested in the effect of military service on earnings.
 The temptation is to estimate:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Earn}_{i}=\alpha_{0}+\alpha_{1}\mbox{Eligible}_{i}+\alpha_{2}\mbox{Educ}_{i}+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

and say 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 gives us the effect of eligibility holding education constant, i.e., the
 effect of military service.
 However, education is endogenous, so the estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

 is likely to be biased because we haven't appropriately controlled for
 the effect of education on earnings.
 Does this mean that we have no hope of isolating the effect of military
 service with this variation? 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can estimate the effect of eligibility on education, i.e., regress education
 on eligibility.
 If the estimator is neither economically or statistically significant,
 then we can still obtain an unbiased estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 If we know the effect of education on earnings (from other studies), then
 we can combine this with the estimated effect of eligibility on education
 to recover the effect of eligibility on earnings that comes that comes
 from this channel.
 We attribute the remainder to military service.
 This approach is equivalent to using a known 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Another approach would be to consider the range of reasonable 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mbox{Educ}_{i} & = & \gamma_{0}+\gamma_{1}\mbox{Elig}_{i}+e_{i}\\
\mbox{Earn}_{i} & = & \theta_{0}+\theta_{1}\mbox{Elig}_{i}+u_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The effect of eligibility on earnings through education is equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\gamma}_{1}\cdot\hat{\alpha}_{2}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\alpha}_{2}$
\end_inset

 is found from another study.
 So, the effect of military service on earnings could be estimated as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\hat{\theta}_{1}-\hat{\gamma}_{1}\cdot\hat{\alpha}_{2}}{\circ}
\]

\end_inset

where the denominator is the effect of eligibility on military service.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can also solve the endogeneity problem in terms of instrumental variables:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Earn}_{i}=\zeta_{0}+\zeta_{1}\mbox{MilitaryService}_{i}+\zeta_{2}\mbox{Educ}_{i}+v_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Here, we have two endogenous variables but only one instrument, i.e., eligibility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Good Controls
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Good controls are fixed characteristics and variables measured before treatment
 is assigned.
 In the example above, controlling for the variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 that is affected by treatment does not give us the effect of training that
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

is not explained by 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Proxy Variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A proxy variable is related to, but not identical to, the variable of interest.
 Even if if they are affected by treatment, they are not necessarily bad
 controls.
 In fact, contolling for a proxy variable influenced by treatment may increase
 or decrease the bias depending on
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The degree to which there is omitted variable bias otherwise
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The degree to which the proxy is correlated with the omitted variable
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The degree to which the proxy is affected by treatment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we want to identify the effect of education on earnings.
 The problem is that those with great innate ability obtain more educaiton
 because it is easier.
 Thus, we need to condition on ability to get estimate right.
 Consider the true model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Earn}_{i}=\alpha+\rho S_{i}+\gamma a_{i}+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

 represent years of schooling and ability for person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, respectively.
 However, we lack any variable that measures ability.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Instead, we omit 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

 from the regression and estimate
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Earn}_{i}=\psi_{1}+\psi_{2}S_{i}+u_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Then, we obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\psi}_{2} & = & \frac{\mbox{Cov}(S_{i},\mbox{Earn}_{i})}{\mbox{Var}(S_{i})}\\
 & = & \frac{\mbox{Cov}(S_{i},\alpha+\rho S_{i}+\gamma a_{i}+\epsilon_{i})}{\mbox{Var}(S_{i})}\\
 & = & 0+\rho+\gamma\frac{\mbox{Cov}(S_{i},a_{i})}{\mbox{Var}(S_{i})}+0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, suppose we observe test scores at age 18.
 This variable could be used as a proxy for 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

, but it may be affected by 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{i}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
t_{i}=\pi_{0}+\pi_{1}S_{i}+\pi_{2}a_{i}+u_{i}
\]

\end_inset

So, we estimate
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Earn}_{i}=\theta_{1}+\theta_{2}S_{i}+\theta_{3}t_{i}+e_{i}
\]

\end_inset

One should recognize that the true model can be reformulated by simply rearrangi
ng terms, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a_{i}=\frac{t_{i}-\pi_{0}-\pi_{1}S_{i}}{\pi_{2}}
\]

\end_inset

This implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Earn}_{i}=\alpha+\rho S_{i}+\gamma\frac{t_{i}-\pi_{0}-\pi_{1}S_{i}}{\pi_{2}}+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

After collecting terms, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Earn}_{i}=\left(\alpha-\gamma\frac{\pi_{0}}{\pi_{2}}\right)+\left(\rho-\gamma\frac{\pi_{1}}{\pi_{2}}\right)S_{i}+\frac{\gamma}{\pi_{2}}t_{i}+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Clearly, the estimates will be biased because
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\theta_{2} & = & \rho-\gamma\frac{\pi_{1}}{\pi_{2}}\\
\hat{\theta}_{2} & \rightarrow & \rho-\gamma\frac{\pi_{1}}{\pi_{2}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The magnitude of the bias depends on the strength of the link between: ability
 and schooling; the proxy and the omitted variabled; the proxy variable
 and schooling.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Dealing With Bias
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What do we do if bias is present? Well, is there anything to learn despite
 these biases? In some situations, biased estimators can provide useful
 information.
 Here, priors suggest:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Estimate without controlling for ability.
 Then, we have bias upwards.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Estimate using the proxy.
 Then, we have bias downwards because all of 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma,\pi_{1},\pi_{2}$
\end_inset

 are greater than 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, the true estimate would lie somewhere in between.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As a rule, biased estimates are generally disliked.
 Though, under certain circumstances, researchers are somtimes tolerable
 of bias if it leads to conservative estimates.
 There are two types of empirical papers:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Those that care about the signs of the estimates, or if the magnitudes are
 greater than or less than a particular benchmark 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Those that care about the magnitudes of the estimates.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The type of paper you are writing depends on the research question:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If you are testing a theory, then we only need to have predictions for signs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For policy evaluations, want to know if benefits are greater than the costs.
 If conservative estimates show that benefits exceed costs, then we know
 that, in reality, benefits would definitely exceed costs.
\end_layout

